20	 ## The run the deck is on
21	 
22	 The deck presses through the page that holds the run, so it only ever sees a run that is synced to your account and open on a page somewhere: the app in a browser tab, a [dock](#guide/dock), or a browser source. With nothing synced the keys say **Not synced**; with runs synced but none open, **No run open**.
23	 
24	 **Run** names the run the deck is following. Press it to cycle through the runs open to you and pin the next one; its settings list the same runs by name. A pin holds: a pinned run that ends says **That run has ended** rather than moving the deck to whichever run went next. Where one run is open and nothing is pinned, the deck follows that one; where several are open and none is pinned, the keys say **Pick a run**.
25	 
26	 ## The keys
27	 
28	 - **Do the next thing** presses whatever the run is waiting on — rolling the table, carrying on, closing the unit, entering the next — and carries the step's own words. Where the step waits on a checklist it ticks everything and presses the step's own button; where it waits on a name, it takes the first suggestion. Tick **Stop at decisions** in its settings and it leaves both to the page.
29	 - **Press** does the one thing you pick in its settings: a move the pack offers, the roll that is waiting, or a preset answer. A setup from your library can be a key too; pressing it changes the run's loadout and hands it out to the tool.
30	-- **Undo** takes back the last result, and says underneath what it would take back.
31	+- **Undo** takes back the last result. It is lit when there is something to take back and dim when there is not.
32	 - **Metric** shows one number and nothing else: the score, the unit, a clock, the last result, the leader, or a counter or resource the pack keeps.
33	 
34	 On a Stream Deck +, Do the next thing and Metric sit on a dial as well as a key. Turning a Metric dial steps through the score, the unit, the clock, the last result and the leader, and a running clock redraws every second.
35	 
36	 A press the run has moved past, or one the page has to take, comes back refused: the key says why for three seconds, then goes back to what it was saying.
37	 
38	 ## Keeping the page awake
39	 
40	 Chrome discards tabs it thinks nobody is looking at, and a discarded tab is a run the deck can no longer see. Keep the run in an OBS dock or a browser source, or exempt Runlog from Memory Saver in the browser's settings.
